The invoice has not reached the customer. The email to m.gruber@huber-bau.at is still stuck in the outgoing queue. The mail server returned "452 4.2.2 over quota" from mx1.huber-bau.at at 10:49:19, and the message is on attempt 9 of 12 with the next retry in 4 hours. Delivery tracking shows no update in 71 hours. The ledger marks the invoice as SENT and the CRM logs an "invoice sent" activity, but those entries reflect the send being queued, not delivery. The CRM also flags the contact address with a soft-bounce status, which is consistent with the deferral.

The invoice details are correct: the amount is € 6,267.22, the due date 2026-11-22 matches 30 days from 2026-10-23, and the PDF is attached. The intended effect, the billing contact receiving the invoice, has not happened, so the work item has not achieved its goal.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "The invoice email to m.gruber@huber-bau.at is still deferred in the queue (recipient mailbox over quota, 71h without delivery), so the customer has not received it despite the ledger showing SENT."}

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "The invoice reached l.lindner@kraus-it.at with the PDF, but it was issued for € 684.99 (subject, ledger, and payment link) instead of the required € 6,849.86, so the customer received an incorrect amount."}

The delivery itself succeeded. SMTP shows the message accepted by mx1.kraus-it.at at 10:57:26 after an earlier greylist deferral, and the delivery tracking confirms it reached the mailbox of l.lindner@kraus-it.at with invoice_INV-2026-1354.pdf attached. The CRM also logs the send to K-50.

The amount is wrong everywhere it appears. The email subject reads "€ 684.99", the ledger posts INV-2026-1354 to K-50 at € 684.99, and the payment link is pay.nordwerk-gmbh.at/INV-2026-1354 for € 684.99. The 14-day terms are correct, since 2026-10-23 plus 14 days gives the 2026-11-06 due date. The agent's "All checks green" reflects successful transport, not correct content. The customer was sent a payment request for a tenth of the amount owed, so the work item's intended effect was not achieved.

The invoice was delivered. The mail log shows it was queued and accepted by the recipient's server (250 2.0.0), and the delivery tracking confirms delivery to p.wagner@steiner-holz.de. The attached PDF (invoice_INV-2026-1948.pdf) and the amount (€ 8,592.15) match the work item. The ledger shows INV-2026-1948 posted to customer K-94 with status SENT, and the CRM logs an "invoice sent" activity on K-94 to the same address. The archive holds the 3-page PDF, and a dunning reminder is scheduled for 2026-11-29, which is consistent with a sent invoice.

Minor gaps: the logs show times but not dates, so I couldn't confirm the send date matches the 2026-10-23 invoice date. I also didn't independently check that steiner-holz.de is K-94's official billing address, though the CRM record ties K-94 to that address.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "Mail log, delivery tracking, ledger, and CRM all confirm invoice INV-2026-1948 (€ 8,592.15, PDF attached) was accepted and delivered to K-94's contact at steiner-holz.de."}

The output does not show the intended effect. The raw file has 24,015 rows and 100 duplicate respondents, so removing only duplicates should leave 23,915 rows. The validation report shows 23,972 rows and says 43 duplicates were removed. That means either 57 duplicate rows remain, or 57 non-duplicate rows were dropped, which the task forbids. Either way the row-count result doesn't match the specification.

The other checks pass: the column count is 61, the weights sum to the target population of 2,195,342, and the female share matches census. The report also doesn't show that the remaining rows are free of duplicates, so the deduplication step is unverified and inconsistent with the 100-duplicate count.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "Only 43 rows were removed instead of the 100 duplicates identified (expected 23,915 rows, got 23,972), so either duplicates remain or non-duplicate rows were dropped."}

The invoice was not delivered to the intended contact. The work item specified billing contact p.pichler@steiner-holz.de, but the mail log shows the message was sent to s.gruber@steiner-holz.de. The agent's "All checks green" report doesn't address this mismatch.

The rest of the message is correct: the amount is €8,793.57, the PDF is attached, and the due date of 2026-12-22 matches 60 days from 2026-10-23. The mail was also accepted by the relay. But the recipient is wrong, so the intended effect (the invoice reaching the billing contact) was not achieved. The fix is to resend to p.pichler@steiner-holz.de and to check why the wrong address was used.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.95, "reason": "The invoice was sent to s.gruber@steiner-holz.de instead of the specified billing contact p.pichler@steiner-holz.de, though the amount, attachment and due date were correct."}

The invoice was sent and the effect is in place. The mail log shows it queued at 10:18:13 and accepted by the recipient server (mx1.steiner-holz.de) at 10:58:30. Delivery tracking confirms it reached the mailbox of s.gruber@steiner-holz.de with invoice_INV-2026-1305.pdf attached. The ledger has INV-2026-1305 posted to customer K-30 for €8,793.57 with status SENT, and the CRM logs an "invoice sent" activity on K-30 to the same address. The PDF is archived.

The one thing not shown directly is the invoice date of 2026-10-23. The due date of 2026-12-22 is consistent with 60-day terms from that date, so I'm treating it as matching.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "Mail logs, delivery tracking, ledger status SENT, and the CRM activity all confirm the invoice with the correct amount and PDF reached customer K-30's mailbox."}

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "The invoice reached a.kraus@huber-bau.at after a greylist deferral, but it states a due date of 2026-11-22 (30 days) instead of the required 60-day terms for K-10, which should give 2026-12-22, and the ledger and dunning schedule carry the same wrong date."}

The delivery itself is confirmed. The SMTP log shows the message was greylisted at 10:38:55 and then accepted by mx1.huber-bau.at at 10:51:13. Delivery tracking shows it reached the a.kraus mailbox, and the ledger shows INV-2026-1170 as SENT with the attachment archived.

The failure is the due date. The runbook says K-10 uses 60-day terms. Counting 60 days from 2026-10-23 gives 2026-12-22. The email subject, the ledger entry, and the 2026-11-29 dunning reminder all use 2026-11-22, which is 30 days. The customer was told the wrong payment deadline, and the reminder fires about three weeks before the real due date. The agent's "All checks green" missed this because the checks confirm sending, not the content.

The invoice reached the customer. The SMTP log shows the message to a.kraus@huber-bau.at was accepted by the recipient's server (mx1.huber-bau.at, 250 2.0.0) at 10:51:13, after an initial greylisting deferral. The delivery tracker reports it delivered to that mailbox, with the PDF attachment and payment link present. The ledger shows INV-2026-1170 posted to K-10 for € 3,626.23, due 2026-11-22, and status SENT. The CRM logs an 'invoice sent' activity on K-10 with the same recipient and attachment, and the PDF is archived.

Two minor loose ends: a duplicate contact merge is suggested for K-10, and I did not check the runbook for whether a.kraus is the correct billing contact for K-10. Neither undermines the send itself.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.87, "reason": "The invoice PDF was accepted by the recipient mail server and delivered to a.kraus@huber-bau.at, and the ledger and CRM both record INV-2026-1170 as sent to K-10."}

The invoice was delivered. The SMTP log shows the first attempt greylisted (421 4.7.0) at 10:38:55, but the same message was accepted by `mx1.huber-bau.at` at 10:51:13. Delivery tracking confirms it reached the mailbox of a.kraus@huber-bau.at with a 2.0.0 status, and the PDF attachment and payment link are present. The ledger shows INV-2026-1170 posted to K-10 for € 3,626.23 with status SENT, due 2026-11-22, and a CRM "invoice sent" activity to the same address with the PDF attached. The archive copy and a dunning reminder are also scheduled.

The agent's "all checks green" was not backed by any output, but the underlying system records support that the send succeeded. The one thing I could not verify is that a.kraus@huber-bau.at is the correct contact for K-10, since the ledger does not show the customer's address. The CRM does flag a duplicate contact merge for K-10, which is worth a look but does not change the outcome.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.88, "reason": "SMTP relay accepted the greylisted retry, delivery tracking confirms mailbox delivery with the PDF, and the ledger and CRM record INV-2026-1170 as sent to K-10 for the correct amount."}

The invoice was delivered to the right customer. The log shows message 2E8D997DB3 sent from billing@nordwerk-gmbh.at to l.wagner@kraus-it.at, which the runbook lists as K-19's billing contact. The recipient's server accepted it (250 2.0.0), and the invoice PDF is attached. The subject carries the correct amount and due date: 2026-10-23 plus the 45-day K-19 term is 2026-12-07.

The one thing the log cannot confirm is the payment link. The runbook says every invoice email contains pay.nordwerk-gmbh.at/INV-2026-1989, but the log shows only headers and attachments, not the body. The agent's "All checks green" report doesn't say whether it checked this either. That is a minor gap, not a reason to call the delivery failed.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.85, "reason": "The log shows the invoice PDF was accepted by the recipient's server at the K-19 billing contact with the correct amount and due date; only the payment link in the body is unverified."}

The invoice reached the intended recipient. Message 79A2CE5EAD went from billing@nordwerk-gmbh.at to s.lindner@berger-moebel.de, and the subject shows the right invoice number, amount and due date. The attachments include invoice_INV-2026-1326.pdf. The spam score (3.1/5.0) was below threshold, and the relay to mx1.berger-moebel.de returned "250 2.0.0 accepted". The due date also checks out: 2026-10-23 plus 45 days is 2026-12-07.

The 14-minute gap between queuing (10:38:22) and relay (10:52:40) is slow but harmless. The other messages in the log are for different recipients and don't affect this item. Acceptance by the recipient's MX is the strongest evidence available here, though it doesn't confirm the mail reached a human's inbox.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "The log shows the correct invoice email with the PDF to s.lindner@berger-moebel.de, passing spam filtering and accepted by the recipient's mail server, with the due date correctly computed."}

The invoice reached the right person. Customer K-26's billing contact in the runbook is s.lindner@berger-moebel.de, which matches the recipient of message 79A2CE5EAD. The subject carries the correct invoice number and amount. The due date checks out: 23 October 2026 plus 45 days (K-26's terms) is 7 December 2026, as the subject states. The message carried invoice_INV-2026-1326.pdf, and the recipient's server accepted it at 10:52:40 with "250 2.0.0 accepted". The spam score of 3.1 was below threshold, so it was not held.

The log does not show the message body, so I could not confirm the payment link (pay.nordwerk-gmbh.at/INV-2026-1326) or the standard English template. The agent's "All checks green" is also vague about what was checked. Those gaps lower my confidence somewhat but do not change the verdict. The other log entries are for different recipients and are not part of this work item.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.85, "reason": "The invoice PDF was accepted by the recipient server for K-26's billing contact, with the correct number, amount and due date, though the body and payment link were not visible in the log."}

The deployment appears to have achieved its intended effect. The process is online and serving traffic, and the config matches the runbook: port 8443 behind nginx, a 20-connection DB pool (20/20 ready), and all 3 cache shards connected. The 119 requests in the last 60 seconds is inside the expected ~112–168/min range (140 ±20%), though it is near the low end and comes from a single sample.

Gaps I couldn't close from this output: the logs don't print the running version, so the v2.3.2 identity rests on the release path, and the agent didn't check the error log or a health endpoint. Those gaps lower my confidence somewhat, but nothing in the evidence points to a failure.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.8, "reason": "The new release is online with the expected port, DB pool, and all 3 cache shards, and traffic (119/min) is within the ~140/min ±20% baseline, though the running version is inferred from the release path rather than confirmed."}

The deployment looks like it achieved its intended effect. The runbook confirms the port (8443 behind nginx), the DB pool size (20), and the cache shard count (3) for notify-worker. The log shows production config loaded, all three resources connected, and the listener up on 8443. The process is freshly restarted (441s uptime) and online under the new release path, `2.3.2-a24d62e`. Traffic is 119 requests in 60s, inside the ~140/min baseline ±20% (112–168), so the service is taking real load.

Gaps: the output doesn't directly show the running process's working directory or nginx routing to the new instance, and the "checks green" claim rests on the script's own output rather than an independent health probe. These are small and don't change the conclusion.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.87, "reason": "Production notify-worker v2.3.2 is online on the runbook-specified port, with correct DB pool, cache shards, and traffic within the expected baseline."}

The runbook matches what I observed: 3 cache shards, a DB pool of 20, port 5000 behind nginx, and a baseline of ~720 requests/min. The observed 686/min is within that band.

The deployment is live and healthy: the right version and commit are running, the instance serves traffic, and it is connected to all three cache shards. The only blemish is one health-probe timeout right after restart, which cleared on retry. I didn't check the nginx front end, but the service is reachable on its port.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.85, "reason": "search-indexer 4.9.5 (3062ed5) is running on port 5000, reports healthy with all 3 cache shards and DB pool connected, and is serving ~686 req/min within the expected ~720 ±20% baseline."}

The work item did not achieve its intended effect. The job log shows the output has 49 columns, but the raw file has 54. The task said nothing else may be dropped, so the 5 missing columns are a direct violation. The agent's "All checks green" report doesn't account for this, and the log reports the drop without flagging it.

The rest checks out. Chunk sizes sum to 23,185 (19 × 1,159 + 1,164), and 530 duplicates were dropped, which should leave 22,655 rows. The weights were rescaled to the stated target of 6,629,453. The three future interview dates were flagged, not changed, and the -9 income codes were kept per the codebook. None of this offsets the lost columns. I did not verify the row count or weight totals in the output file itself, since only the log was provided.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "The output has 49 columns versus 54 in the raw file, so 5 columns were dropped despite the explicit requirement that nothing else be removed."}
